1. A business may use, copy and change Foundation, and build on it, to
   make and run software for itself, free of charge. "The business",
   below, is the business whose software it is; a person building for
   themselves is one too.

2. The business makes its own copy of Foundation, in a repository it
   owns, and holds it. It may have people it hires, like a developer or
   an agency, work on that copy for it, under these same terms. They
   work on the business's copy, never on one of their own, and keep
   nothing of it for any other use.

3. What the business adds to Foundation is its own. It may run what it
   builds as its own website, app or service, and sell what that does
   to its own customers. Running it is not handing it over.

4. Apart from what term 2 allows, nobody may hand Foundation, or
   anything made from it, to anyone else: not as a template, starter
   kit, boilerplate or other base for other people's software, and not
   as software built for someone else from a copy of their own.

5. Every copy of Foundation, or of a substantial part of it, keeps this
   license with it. Software built on Foundation keeps the line in its
   pages' source that names Superforge, the "generator" tag that
   source/utilities/template.ts sets, unless Superforge has agreed in
   writing that it may go.

6. These terms cover release 0.33.0 of Foundation and every release
   after it; the releases before 0.33.0 were published under the MIT
   license. They pass to whoever takes over the business, and last as
   long as the business keeps to them.

